  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1113 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nFog trends through Friday morning...Cold front over northern WI\nwill stall, wash out over WI on Thursday. Winds behind the front\nswitch to NE which will bring lake moisture back into mix for the\nfog. Later tonight, will see fog develop, mainly shallow/ground\ntype we saw early Wednesday morning. However, slug of stratus and\nfog over Lake Superior is forecast to advect into far northeast WI\ntoward daybreak Thursday, spreading to west of the Fox Valley and\nacross Door County/lakeshore. Not sure how much fog occurs in the\nFox Valley on Thursday morning, but that will be something to \nmonitor. Dense fog is likely, but confidence is not there yet to\nissue an advisory. High resolution guidance and blended guidance \nsuggests Marinette into Door County most favored. Given the onshore\nsfc winds and weak flow aloft, patchy fog and stratus could linger\nwell into the afternoon for some of the area.\n\nAfter a brief hiatus in the lower vsby later Thursday, east winds\nand moisture off the lake and bay combined with nighttime cooling\nshould result in more fog Thursday night into early Friday, which\ncould be dense at times. Dense fog pattern eases up on Friday\nnight with increasing winds and warmer temps.   \n\nDry Through Saturday Afternoon: Mid-level ridge stays locked in \nthrough Saturday leading to mostly tranquil and dry weather across\nthe forecast area. Inland high temperatures will reach the upper \n60s to lower 70s, while onshore lake breezes keep it cooler near \nLake Michigan.  \n\nIncreased Rain Chances Saturday Night Through Monday: A northern\nstream shortwave trough advancing across central Canada will push\nan associated cold front across the Upper Midwest Saturday night \ninto Sunday. Moisture pooling along the slow-moving frontal \nboundary will increase rain shower chances to 20 to 40 percent \nacross north-central and northeast Wisconsin Saturday night. Small\nchances linger on Sunday, with a resurgence of scattered showers \nlater Sunday night through Monday. LREF higher than NBM regarding\nextent of chances, but shows up to 70% chance of seeing 0.01 inch\nand up to 50% chance of seeing 0.10 inch. So, still looking like a\nhigher POP/low QPF type of setup with frontal boundary nearby or\njust to the south and right entrance of jet/frontogenesis enhancing\nthe rain chances. Deterministic models show more variability than\nensembles and NBM in location/timing of the steadiest rain, so \nthis will have to be sorted out over the coming days. Beyond Monday,\nsignal persists for a more unsettled weather pattern into the \nmiddle of the week with at least small chances of showers as mid-\nlevel flow becomes more zonal and active with multiple shortwave \ntroughs moving through. \n\nCooler Temperatures to start next week: Post-frontal cold air \nadvection will usher in a significantly cooler Canadian airmass \nMonday through Tuesday, dropping daytime high temperatures into \nthe upper 40s to lower 50s area-wide. It is brief though as \ntemperatures show slight warming trend later next week. Nighttime\nlows will fall back into the 30s.",
